{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '__destruct|type declarations|typed properties|new' collab/1792197000000-architect-yang-ts-php-designs.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md | tail -9 && sed -n '540,556p' docs/design/dao.stream.md\"","aggregated_output":"309:| Operators | arithmetic (overflow to float), string `.`, `.=`, comparison (`==`, `===`, `<=>`, `<` with the juggling table), logical (`&&`, `and` precedence difference is the grammar's), `??`, `??=`, ternary and short ternary, bitwise (int only; on strings bytewise), casts `(int)`/`(string)`/`(array)`/`(object)`/`(bool)`/`(float)`, `instanceof`, `clone`, `new`, error-suppression `@` (diagnostics muted for the expression), increment/decrement (string and null rules), `print` as expression | `php/*` applications |\n313:| Functions | declarations (hoisted or in place), closures with `use`, arrow fns, defaults, by-ref params, variadics, named args, nullable/union type declarations with coercion or `TypeError`, return types, `static` vars, `global`, first-class callable `f(...)`, callables | `php/make-function name spec code statics`; calls `php/call callee args named-args class-ctx` |\n359:| request scope (new row,    | slots; objects are cell handles; locals are      |            |\n393:| P0 | Grammar package `antlr/php/manifest.edn`, worker profile, CST export of modes (inline HTML, heredoc, interpolation), licence block | packets for mixed HTML/code with byte spans; `?>` newline rule preserved; heredoc tokens span their source; refusal of the generics syntax |\n397:| P4 | Classes, interfaces, traits, enums, `match`, exceptions, magic methods, visibility, typed properties and declarations (coercive and strict), named args; generators on the C2 core | classes and exceptions groups; a `TypeError` for `f(\"x\")` with `int $x` under `strict_types=1` and coercion under 0; `instanceof` across units of one task |\n401:| P8 | Legacy retirement: the `php_test.clj` programs in the new corpus, consumers retargeted, `yang/php.cljc` deleted, `:yang.php/legacy` removed from the standard catalog | REPL `<?php $x = 1; echo $x;` prints `1`; all prior PHP test programs pass with corrected expectations; no reference to `yang.php` remains |\n404:Deferred: `preg_*` (portable regex engine), `mb_*`, `serialize`, Reflection, SPL data structures, `DateTime` beyond the time effect, sessions, PDO and every `ext/*` surface (bound per ADR 0004), return by reference, `goto`, `__destruct`.\n406:**yang.core lift enumeration for PHP** (beyond L1 to L4): nothing new is required; PHP exercises `yang.core.stage` (ok-check on `:yang.cst/ok`), `yang.core.lower` (name supply, diagnostics with `:yang.php` namespace), `yang.core.module` (`module-symbol` with prefix `phpm`, `declare-free-name`), `yang.core.publication` (with `php/imported-unit-names` from the literal `include` edges and the class map), `yang.core.prelude` (emitters, `admit` with `host-names` adding the byte-string `data` exports). If the owner schedules the `yrt` shared prelude (A12.5), PHP is the second consumer of its escape machinery, generator core, ordered map and module skeleton; until then PHP copies the patterns under `php/` as TS does under `js/`.\n451:- Documentation amendments the designs imply: §3.2/§2.1/§8.5.2 stale text (D3, D4); §8.3 new rows (A10, B5); §8.5's open module-variable question answered per language (D6); §8.6 and §8.7 superseded in detail by these designs once accepted; `yang.antlr.md`'s implementation-status paragraph to say JavaScript is unparked as TypeScript.\n  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n  works with that interpreter's own handle. A cursor is plain data and\n  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n  same rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names is\n  a cursor like any other: `next` answers `cursor-mismatch` when the handle\n  it is given is on a different logical stream, never a defect. A descriptor\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
