{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-17 08:11:13 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-17 15:11:13 +0700\n\nReviewed the complete revision against HEAD `5f8963093631ab447840cd6a041d83fadccf3999`; tracked files match HEAD. No files edited. Verification used committed source and Zend’s handlers; PHP CLI was not run.\n\nThe three round-4 reference counterexamples now trace correctly. Saved stream-minted cursors, acquisition on the first late claim, and the narrowed import-ordering claim address the other round-4 findings. The PHP table still collapses several materially different handler branches:\n\n1. **[P1] `FE_FETCH_RW` must not repeat the separating write fetch.**  \n   [Row 12](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:94) routes each element through row 5:\n   ```php\n   $a = [1, 2];\n   foreach ($a as &$v) {\n       if ($v === 1) $b = $a;\n       $v += 10;\n   }\n   echo $b[1]; // Zend: 12\n   ```\n   Zend separates at `FE_RESET_RW`; `FE_FETCH_RW` subsequently obtains iterator buckets directly. The revision separates again after `$b=$a`, leaving `$b[1]` at `2`. Define iterator-element access separately from ordinary writable-path access, including iterator-position handling across mutations. [Zend foreach handlers](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_vm_def.h).\n\n2. **[P1] Unset cannot share the vivifying `FETCH_W` procedure.**  \n   [Section 1.1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:18) explicitly puts unset on the same path, while `FETCH_W` creates missing keys:\n   ```php\n   $a = [];\n   unset($a['missing']['child']);\n   var_dump($a); // remains empty\n   ```\n   The ruled fetch creates `missing`, changing the array. Zend distinguishes `BP_VAR_UNSET` from write/read-write fetches. Define a non-vivifying unset traversal with missing-path handling; retain separation where applicable. [Zend dimension-fetch implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.c).\n\n3. **[P1] SEND/RETURN still lack temporary ownership and cleanup rules.**  \n   [Rows 1, 13 and 15](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:83) collapse borrowed CV sources and owned temporaries into copying assignment. Zend distinguishes copying from ownership transfer, releases discarded return values, and cleans temporaries on exceptional paths. Releasing CVs alone does not cover these owners.\n\n   A function returning a newly allocated object whose result is discarded must destroy that object before the following statement. The revision creates a caller temporary but specifies no corresponding release. Define temporary acquisition, transfer and cleanup—including unfinished argument lists—or an equivalent balanced copy/release scheme. [Zend SEND/RETURN handlers](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_vm_def.h).\n\n4. **[P1] References to typed properties lose their persistent constraints.**  \n   The [reference representation and assignment procedure](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:37) retain only count and value:\n   ```php\n   class C { public int $x = 1; }\n   $o = new C;\n   $r =& $o->x;\n   $r = \"bad\"; // TypeError\n   ```\n   Ordinary assignment through `$r` cannot enforce the property’s constraint using that representation. Zend references retain **type sources**, checked even when assignment occurs through an alias. Specify attachment/removal of these constraints, multiple constrained property aliases, and validation/coercion before mutation. This is required by the retained typed-property support claim. [Zend typed-reference assignment](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.h).\n\n5. **[P1] Object finalization is conflated with freeing storage.**  \n   [DESTROY(obj)](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:49) unconditionally releases properties after `__destruct`. A destructor can resurrect `$this`, for example by saving it in `$GLOBALS`; its properties must then remain alive. Zend records that the destructor ran and rechecks the count before freeing storage. Specify those states and exception-safe pin release. [Zend object-store deletion](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_objects_API.c).\n\n   [Row 22](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792219000000-architect-ts-php-r5.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:104) also releases all globals before remaining destructors. Zend’s shutdown destructor phase preserves scalar globals for destructor access and precedes general executor-value cleanup. A destructor reading an earlier-created global string therefore distinguishes the proposed sequence. Define shutdown separately from ordinary frame release. [Zend shutdown phases](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute_API.c).\n\nThe evidence-anchor race rule is coherent under the stated ledger order: the winning record is published before either producer’s claims can precede its cursor. Correct its terminology to **logical-stream identity**, rather than attachment identity, to match the cursor contract.\n\nThe remaining blockers are in the PHP operation specification. Naming rows after opcodes does not preserve semantics when operand ownership, fetch modes and finalization branches are omitted.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES"}}
